# Our defaults for OpenSpiel's C++ AlphaZero on Thud, where they differ from upstream's.
# The reasoning in full, and the settings still to decide: thud/PLAN.md Phase 6,
# "Settings to determine empirically". From build-torch/:
#
#   OMP_NUM_THREADS=1 ./examples/alpha_zero_torch_example \
#     --flagfile=../thud/experiments/az_thud.flags --path=DIR [more flags]
#
# The last value of a flag wins, so flags after --flagfile override these. Each run's
# config.json records the values it used.
#
# OMP_NUM_THREADS=1 is an environment variable, so it cannot go in this file. LibTorch
# gives every caller 10 threads by default, and each actor and evaluator calls the network
# itself, so they oversubscribe the 10 cores: three searches at once managed 16
# simulations/s each with the default, 128 each with one thread (measured 2026-09-25).

--game=thud

# Resignation off (decided with the user, 2026-09-25; upstream default 0.8, meaning 80%
# of self-play games may resign). Why:
# - Thud's returns are the final margin over 32, and margins reward playing a decided
#   battle to the best score. A resigned game instead records the search's estimate as
#   its result (alpha_zero.cc:155-157) and skips exactly that part.
# - Upstream's threshold, a value above 0.95, means expecting to win by more than 30 of
#   32 points, so it would rarely fire anyway.
# - Decided battles still end: after 200 turns without a capture or 800 in all
#   (THUD_RULES.md section 6), with real margins. The cost is self-play time in such dead
#   tails; if that proves large, lower the no-capture cap rather than resign.
# 0 turns it off completely: a game may resign only if a uniform draw in [0, 1) is below
# this value (alpha_zero.cc:202-203). Evaluation games never resign.
--cutoff_probability=0

# Dirichlet noise at the root: alpha 0.1 (decided with the user, 2026-09-25; upstream
# default 1). Why:
# - AlphaZero scaled alpha "in inverse proportion to the approximate number of legal
#   moves in a typical position" (0.3 chess, 0.15 shogi, 0.03 Go; arXiv 1712.01815).
#   Alpha times the move count, roughly how many moves the noise lands on, stays near 10.
# - Thud's two sides differ greatly: the dwarfs have ~400 legal moves, the trolls ~32. By
#   AlphaZero's rule that is alpha ~0.025 for the dwarfs and ~0.3 for the trolls, but the
#   C++ bot applies one alpha to whichever side is to move (mcts.cc:284-290).
# - Upstream's 1 gives the dwarfs ~400: noise spread evenly, like mixing in 25% uniform,
#   so no untried idea ever stands out. 0.1, the geometric mean of the two sides'
#   values, gives ~40 for the dwarfs and ~3 for the trolls: 4x and 3x off the rule.
# Still to test: 0.03 and 0.3 against 0.1, once uct_c and the simulations are chosen.
--policy_alpha=0.1
